nt.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_uncertified_chunks_close.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "cannot_certify is not memoized per fact, so a dirty-tree fork-point fact (M10 shape) that both names a chunk and spans re-runs _merged_verbatim's --ancestry-path log and per-commit rev-lists on every Stop", "recommendation": "instance. Why it broke: chunk_states calls cannot_certify(fact, body) once in the naming loop and again in the spanning loop for the same fact. _Reachability memoizes the anchor probes and the first-parent rev-list per anchor, but _merged_verbatim (one `git log --ancestry-path` plus a `first_parent_answer` rev-list per commit carrying the reviewed tree) is recomputed on each call. The answer is still correct; the cost is double what the NF1 docstring's 'one --ancestry-path log and a rev-list per commit carrying its reviewed tree' implies, and G2 pins only the M2 shape. Memoizing cannot_certify by fact id (or _merged_verbatim by (anchor, tree)) closes it. The cost is already covered by the accepted per-Stop cost (R-13), so no edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Two independent definitions of 'the dirty-tree review's tree, committed verbatim on a first-parent chain' (_Reachability._merged_verbatim and _Spans._verbatim_chain) with different selection rules", "recommendation": "Both answer the same question for the same dirty-tree fact: which commit carries head_tree verbatim on a first-parent chain from the anchor or dispatch commit. _merged_verbatim walks backward from any commit in the ancestry-path log with that tree, accepts any match, and then requires it to be off the base's first-parent history. _verbatim_chain runs a BFS forward from dispatch_commit, takes the nearest match, prefers a non-merge commit only within one BFS depth, and applies no first-parent-of-base filter. Today they agree on the shapes the case table pins. They are still two copies of one rule, and a later change to it, say the merge preference or the 'edited again before committed' clause, can land in one and not the other. Reachability would then accept an anchor on one commit while the span credits a chain ending at a different one, so the chunk's done/open state and its reason text disagree with the rule the docstrings state. This is the same one-owner property that cannot_certify establishes for refusals in this change. Extract one helper that returns the verbatim chain from a start commit, given a commit log and a tree. _Spans uses the chain; _merged_verbatim checks that the chain's last commit is off the base's first-parent history.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "A git failure inside _merged_verbatim is reported as 'the base held it', contrary to the _Reachability docstring", "recommendation": "The _Reachability docstring says 'When git cannot answer the first-parent question the anchor is refused, and the reason says git failed.' That holds for the anchor's own first_parent_answer. It does not hold inside _merged_verbatim: a failed --ancestry-path log returns False, and so does a per-commit first_parent_answer that returns a failure string (the `is False` test fails on a str). Either way the anchor falls through to the 'review's own base commit ... the base held it before the review ran' refusal. The chunk still fails closed, so state is correct and only the stated cause is wrong for M10's shape when git fails. This is in the same family as the accepted R-11 and R-12, but it is new code and a separate claim. A fix would return the failure from _merged_verbatim and name it in the refusal. Otherwise narrow the docstring sentence to the anchor's own probe.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner (Goal 5 roll-up)", "recommendation": "Scope none. These are recorded in the plan's 'Decisions made mid-build', and each one is settled there. (1) Span credit comes only from scoped `(Chunk N)` subjects on first-parent commits of the fact's own interval. This was settled by the owner's ruling on #1082 (issue comment 6456663316), which removed path credit after three review rounds. (2) Only a coverage edge may span (coverage_algebra.review_edges). (3) A dirty-tree span counts its uncommitted part only once that tree is committed verbatim. (4) M5 (fast-forward with anchor = base_commit) and M6 (squash) stay open, and the consumer note discloses both. (5) The proposal keeps its path half, under the one-owner rule. (6) The first-parent rule has one owner, on_first_parent, shared with plan_lifecycle. (7) record_lint now supports bare-path governed_by entries instead of flagging the form. The data-model D1 amendment is a dated extension beneath the statement, and it cites the owner's ruling on GitHub. That satisfies 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority'. No alignment-pass miss was observed. The plan's Goals are at Level High, with no unmarked inferences.", "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: #1079 governance: dirty-tree chunk review reads open after its plan merges", "recommendation": "Backlog item appears resolved: #1079 (rows M2, M3 and M10 pinned in tests/test_chunk_state_table.py; plan_lifecycle shares on_first_parent). Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update 1079 status=shipped closed-by=scope:chunk-state-table` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says.